INSERT OR REPLACE INTO lab_quizzes (id, topic_id, question, options, correct_answer_index, explanation) VALUES 
('q01', 't01', 'What primary characteristic distinguishes "Spear Phishing" from traditional bulk phishing?', '["The malware payload is larger.", "It targets a specific, carefully researched individual.", "It strictly utilizes SMS messaging.", "It requires physical access to a corporate device."]', 1, 'Unlike bulk spam, spear phishing utilizes OSINT to create a highly personalized, targeted attack designed specifically to bypass the individual’s natural skepticism.'),
('q02', 't02', 'Which psychological vulnerability is primarily exploited during a Pretexting attack?', '["Inherent greed for free software.", "Fear of missing an emergency deadline.", "Instinctual deference to recognized authority figures.", "Boredom or idle scrolling."]', 2, 'Pretexting relies heavily on social engineering trust. The attacker acts as an authority figure (like IT support or law enforcement) because humans are socially conditioned to comply with authority without questioning it.'),
('q03', 't03', 'Why are "Homograph Attacks", such as substituting a Latin "p" with a Cyrillic "р", uniquely dangerous?', '["They bypass multi-factor authentication entirely.", "They are visually identical to the legitimate URL but point to an attacker-controlled server.", "They crash modern internet browsers.", "They hijack the victim’s local router settings."]', 1, 'Homograph attacks deceive users because the letters look perfectly identical on screen, meaning standard visual checks of the address bar fail to alert the victim that they are on a cloned credential-harvesting server.'),
('q04', 't04', 'What is the primary objective of an "Evil Twin" attack?', '["To physically clone a victim''s hard drive.", "To set up a fraudulent Wi-Fi access point and intercept unencrypted network traffic.", "To duplicate an employee''s RFID building badge.", "To guess local administrator passwords."]', 1, 'By broadcasting a Wi-Fi network with the same SSID as a legitimate hotspot (like "Starbucks WiFi"), attackers trick devices into connecting, allowing the attacker to establish a Man-in-the-Middle position and steal session cookies.'),
('q05', 't05', 'Which defense is most effective against "Quishing" (QR Code Phishing) in public spaces?', '["Always scanning QR codes quickly before they expire.", "Only using the default camera app without a preview function.", "Using secure scanner applications that display the parsed destination URL before redirecting.", "Entering your password to verify the QR code’s authenticity."]', 2, 'Because the human eye cannot decode a QR code visually, using a scanner that previews the destination domain allows you to verify it isn''t pointing to a malicious typosquatted website before the browser loads it.'),
('q06', 't06', 'Why are "Whaling" campaigns exponentially more devastating than standard phishing attacks?', '["They are significantly cheaper for attackers to deploy.", "They target high-profile executives with elevated administrative and financial authorization.", "They encrypt the entire network instantaneously upon delivery.", "They physically damage the targeted network switches."]', 1, 'Whaling specifically targets the "C-Suite" (CEOs, CFOs). A compromised executive account allows threat actors to authorize massive fraudulent wire transfers or bypass internal security reviews entirely due to hierarchical corporate trust.'),
('q07', 't07', 'In a scenario involving "Vishing" powered by Deepfake audio, what is the strongest corporate defense?', '["Hanging up the phone repeatedly.", "Using purely email-based communications.", "Implementing a strict Out-of-Band (OOB) secondary verification channel before executing sensitive requests.", "Running anti-virus software on the phone."]', 2, 'Because Deepfake audio can perfectly mimic an executive’s voice, the only reliable defense is Zero Trust verification—requiring the employee to confirm the urgent verbal command via a separate, secure channel like a company Slack message.'),
('q08', 't08', 'A malicious USB flash drive labeled "Executive Salaries" dropped in a lobby is an example of what social engineering tactic?', '["Spear Phishing", "Quishing", "Typosquatting", "Baiting"]', 3, 'Baiting preys entirely on human curiosity and greed. An attacker doesn''t need to breach the digital firewall if they can trick a curious employee into manually inserting a malware payload directly into an internal workstation.'),
('q09', 't09', 'In the context of cyber reconnaissance, what does OSINT stand for?', '["Optimized Security Internal Networking Tool", "Open Source Intelligence", "Offensive Systems Integration Network", "Operator Status Independent Threat"]', 1, 'OSINT refers to the practice of harvesting publicly available data—from social media, public records, and forum posts—to build highly specific behavioral profiles before launching customized phishing or social engineering attacks.'),
('q10', 't10', 'When mitigating the "Human Element" of cybersecurity, what is the benefit of a "No-Blame" organizational culture?', '["It lowers corporate insurance premiums.", "It encourages employees to rapidly report a successful phishing click rather than hiding it out of fear of punishment.", "It eliminates the need for IT security teams.", "It prevents employees from ever receiving phishing emails."]', 1, 'A No-Blame culture is critical because the speed of incident reporting dictates the severity of the breach. Punishing victims causes them to silently attempt to cover up the mistake, giving attackers critical time to escalate privileges.'),

('q11', 't11', 'Why does modern ransomware utilize Asymmetric public-key cryptography during file encryption?', '["To make the encryption faster than symmetric algorithms.", "To securely encrypt the local symmetric key, preventing the victim from decrypting their files without buying the attacker''s private key.", "To delete files permanently without the ability to restore them.", "To bypass Windows Defender firewalls."]', 1, 'Symmetric keys handle the rapid file encryption, but Asymmetric keys are then used to lock the Symmetric key. Without the attacker’s mathematically linked private key, computing the decryption is mathematically impossible.'),
('q12', 't12', 'What distinguishes a "Double Extortion" ransomware attack from a traditional ransomware incident?', '["The attacker charges twice as much money.", "The attacker encrypts the data twice with two different algorithms.", "The attacker exfiltrates sensitive data before encrypting it, threatening to leak it publicly if the ransom is refused.", "The attacker targets two different branch offices simultaneously."]', 2, 'Double Extortion defeats the "just restore from backups" defense. Even if you can decrypt your files independently, the attacker leverages the stolen unencrypted data by threatening devastating public exposure and regulatory fines.'),
('q13', 't13', 'Under the Ransomware as a Service (RaaS) model, what is the primary role of an "Affiliate"?', '["Developing the complex encryption algorithms.", "Writing the ransomware payment portals.", "Breaching individual corporate perimeters and deploying the payload.", "Hosting the Dark Web infrastructure."]', 2, 'In the RaaS ecosystem, the cartel provides the malware and infrastructure. The un-skilled "Affiliates" act as mercenaries who strictly focus on compromising corporate networks and detonating the payload in exchange for a percentage of the ransom profit.'),
('q14', 't14', 'Initial Access Brokers (IABs) specialize in selling which specific commodity to ransomware cartels?', '["Zero-Day exploits for web browsers.", "Persistent, administrative access to already-compromised corporate networks.", "Cryptocurrency tumbling services.", "Stolen credit card numbers."]', 1, 'IABs do not execute ransomware themselves. They locate weak networks, establish a silent persistent foothold (e.g., via stolen VPN credentials), and auction that direct access, allowing ransomware affiliates to completely skip the difficult initial breach phase.'),
('q15', 't15', 'During a network intrusion, what is the goal of "Lateral Movement"?', '["Pivoting from an initially compromised endpoint to increasingly privileged systems, aiming for Domain Admin access.", "Downloading files sequentially from a single workstation.", "Encrypting files faster using multiple CPU cores.", "Moving the physical location of the attacker server."]', 0, 'Attackers rarely land exactly where they need to be. Lateral Movement involves silently scraping memory for credentials and moving horizontally across the network infrastructure until they secure absolute control over the central Active Directory server.'),
('q16', 't16', 'How does the Principle of Least Privilege (PoLP) actively cripple the spread of ransomware?', '["By granting all users equal, unmonitored access.", "By preventing users from accessing the global internet.", "By restricting user permissions solely to the minimum necessary for their job role, preventing malware from executing broadly across network segments.", "By encrypting external hard drives automatically."]', 2, 'If an employee successfully triggers a ransomware payload on an account constrained by PoLP, the malware inherits those exact limitations. It cannot access, encrypt, or completely destroy elevated engineering databases or administrative network segments.'),
('q17', 't17', 'Why do advanced ransomware variants execute commands like `vssadmin delete shadows`?', '["To hide the attacker''s IP address.", "To erase the Windows Volume Shadow Copies, preventing the victim from rolling back their machine to an unencrypted state.", "To clear the Windows Event Logs and erase forensic evidence.", "To disable the workstation''s display monitor."]', 1, 'Microsoft''s native VSS snapshots allow administrators to quickly restore deleted or corrupted files locally. By silently purging these shadow volumes, the ransomware brutally guarantees that entirely independent recovery without the decryption key is impossible.'),
('q18', 't18', 'How did the infamous WannaCry ransomware utilize an unregistered DNS "Killswitch"?', '["It encrypted the DNS server itself.", "It queried a chaotic domain name; if it received a response indicating security analysis, it paradoxically halted all encryption operations.", "It used DNS to tunnel stolen passwords.", "It shut down the victim''s router immediately."]', 1, 'To evade security researchers trying to study the malware in isolated network sandboxes, the ransomware checked if a random domain resolved. If a researcher registered the domain to monitor it, the malware realized it was trapped and self-terminated.'),
('q19', 't19', 'In malware operations, what is "Beaconing"?', '["Flashing the hard drive LED light to signal infection.", "Repeated, low-and-slow cyclic network requests from infected hosts calling out to their Command and Control (C2) server for active instructions.", "Broadcasting Wi-Fi signals to infect nearby devices.", "Sending automated SOS emails to IT."]', 1, 'Beaconing is the heartbeat of the attack. Infected hosts silently and repeatedly "ping" their remote operators—often utilizing Domain Generation Algorithms (DGA) to evade static firewalls—awaiting operational orders or the final encryption payload.'),
('q20', 't20', 'According to the 3-2-1 backup methodology, what represents the ultimate failsafe against complete organizational ransomware destruction?', '["Keeping 3 copies of data exclusively on the local C: drive.", "Maintaining 1 immutable, air-gapped copy of critical data entirely off-site and disconnected from the primary network.", "Using 3 separate cloud providers simultaneously.", "Backing up data every 3 hours natively to an attached USB drive."]', 1, 'Because modern ransomware actively hunts and destroys network-attached storage and cloud-synced databases prior to detonating, an offline, air-gapped, immutable backup is the only mathematical guarantee of surviving the nuclear encryption event.')
